// Lesson 9.6 — Bias, Filtering & Fair Data
// concept-frame CSP lesson: sampling bias, filtering consequences, scoped claims.
//
// GROUND TRUTH — read out of public/data/playtests.csv, not out of this lesson:
//   32 rows, 8 distinct testers (P01–P08), 6 sections. So "who is in the
//   sample" is eight people recorded four times each, not thirty-two opinions.
//   completed_boss = FALSE on exactly 7 rows (R003, R007, R012, R013, R018,
//   R024, R029) — quitters DO leave rows; the players with no row at all are
//   the ones who stopped mid-cave. The survivorship row of the bias table and
//   Build Lab Step 2 now say that, and they used to contradict each other.
//   Mean rating over all 32 runs is 3.81 (the 3.8 quoted in the socratic);
//   over the 25 finishers only it is 4.16 — the survivorship trap, in numbers.
//   Bug Hunt item 2's "average drops 20%" holds for section 5 (167.7s → 133.7s
//   when its three slowest runs are dropped), which is the section it names.
//   NO C# FILE IS REFERENCED IN THIS LESSON. The ".cs" a sweep reported in the
//   Build Lab is playtests.csv, matched on its first three characters — it is
//   the unit's dataset, it lives at public/data/playtests.csv, and the link
//   resolves. The only code-shaped thing here is the AP-pseudocode fix in Step
//   3 (arrow + DISPLAY notation, the same shape 9.5 Step 3 uses); its lead-in
//   now says outright that it does not compile or run.
//
// SHAPE: concept frame (src/lib/walkthrough.ts). There is no file to hand over
// in Unit 9, so the banner and the decompose block both carry data-scaffold="3"
// and drop the visible code chip — "Outline only" is a claim about a file this
// lesson does not have. Level 3 is the only reachable rung for a lesson with no
// walkthrough (verify-site check 7).
//
// The decompose block is ADDED, not substituted: it opens a new answer-id
// namespace (9.6:dc1:qN) and leaves socratic, bug-hunt and tinker in their
// document positions with their item counts unchanged (1, 3, 2), so every
// saved answer keeps the id it already had. Reworded in place, same count and
// same position: the socratic (it quoted a sample size and an average that the
// dataset does not support) and the survivorship row of the bias table.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';
import type { DecomposeSpec } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big:
    'Decide whether your playtest data is fair. The answer is not in the numbers — it is in who is ' +
    'missing from the table, and in what you quietly removed before you looked at it.',
  frame: 'concept',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Name who is actually in the table',
      does:
        'Count the `player_id` column rather than the rows: 32 rows in this dataset came from eight ' +
        'people, so a "sample of 32" is really eight opinions recorded four times each.',
      doc: 'the `player_id` and `run_id` columns of the dataset you read in 9.5, and the pool audit in Step 1 of the Build Lab below',
      href: '9.5.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Say what population that sample stands in for',
      does:
        'Write the scope sentence — "this data represents ___, and does not represent ___" — because ' +
        'every claim you make afterwards inherits the population you name here.',
      doc: 'the scope sentence you write in Step 1 of the Build Lab below',
    },
    {
      name: 'Find the players the table has no row for',
      does:
        'A row with `completed_boss` = FALSE is a player who quit and still left data; the players who ' +
        'stopped mid-cave left nothing at all, and that absence is what makes the averages look friendly.',
      doc: 'the `completed_boss` column of the dataset, and the absences you list in Step 2 of the Build Lab below',
    },
    {
      name: 'Watch what a filter removes before you look at what it keeps',
      does:
        'Dropping "outliers" is a decision about people, so an average has to be reported next to the ' +
        'count it threw away.',
      doc: 'the filtered average in Step 3 of the Build Lab below, and the same move in 9.5',
      href: '9.5.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Write the claim at the strength the data can carry',
      does:
        'The exam asks which claim is BEST supported, and the winner is always the one whose scope ' +
        'clause matches the sample you actually have.',
      doc: 'the "which claim is BEST supported" practice in the Concept Lab above',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The split is the whole lesson. "Is this data fair?" is one question you can only answer by feel; ' +
    'these five are five checks you can run in order, and each one has somewhere to read about it. ' +
    'Nothing here needs a program — fairness lives in the sample and in what you chose to drop, which ' +
    'is why the exam asks about claims rather than about code.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you defend the claim with only the table in front of you? A part that needs your ' +
    'memory of who playtested, rather than a column you can point at, is not part of the data problem.',
  turns: [
    'Thirty-two rows is not thirty-two opinions. Rewrite one of yesterday\'s insights with its scope clause attached, and say which half of the sentence got weaker and which half got honest.',
    'Name one filter on this dataset that would raise the average rating, and say what a report would have to print next to that average to keep it honest.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>9.6 — Bias, Filtering &amp; Fair Data</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Yesterday's dataset looked scientific. Today the harder question:
    WHO is in it? Data can be perfectly collected and perfectly wrong,
    because the sample — not the numbers — carries the bias. This is
    the IOC-1 core idea on the exam, and it starts with your own
    playtest pool.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I tell whether data is fair?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('9.6', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Sampling bias, in your room.</strong> Who playtested
    your cave? Your friends, your table, people who already play
    platformers. If the class's data says "section 3 is easy," but
    every tester has 200 hours of Hollow Knight — is section 3
    easy? The data is TRUE about the SAMPLE and misleading about the
    PLAYERS WHO WILL ACTUALLY PLAY. That gap is sampling bias.</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Bias</th><th>In your project</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Selection bias</strong></td><td>Playtesters who volunteered are more forgiving (self-selection)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Survivorship bias</strong></td><td>You count the players who stayed to the end. The ones who quit mid-cave left no row at all</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Observer effect</strong></td><td>Testers play differently when the designer watches (<a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> — why we sit silently)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Confirmation bias</strong></td><td>You tune what you already suspected was broken</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Filtering — the exam's favorite trap.</strong> A
    filter is a question you ask data: "show me only section 5."
    Filtering helps see patterns; it can also silently erase
    people. The classic: "let's drop the outlier runs" — until you
    realize the dropped runs are all the players who quit, and
    your average difficulty just got friendlier for no
    reason.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The dataset holds 32 runs from 8 testers, and the runs
            that never beat the boss are still sitting in it — which
            is why the whole table averages 3.8 out of 5. Somebody
            wants to publish "players gave the cave 4 out of 5." The
            exam asks: "Which claim is BEST supported?" — write the
            cautious version AND the overreaching version of that
            sentence, and say why the exam always wants the first.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — audit the playtest pool.</strong> For the
    <a href="../data/playtests.csv">playtests.csv</a> dataset (or
    your own <a href="7.7.html">7.7</a> table): list who was IN
    the sample and what kind of player was NOT. Write one sentence
    of scope: "This data represents ___, and does not represent
    ___."</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — find the missing rows.</strong> The dataset
    has completed_boss = FALSE rows. Who is ABSENT entirely? Players
    who stopped playing mid-cave — no row at all. For each of these
    absences, name the conclusion that the data would silently get
    wrong, and say which column you would have to start recording to
    stop it.</p>

    <details class="wt-final wt-gated">
        <summary>
            <span class="wt-gated-title">Check your answers</span>
            <span class="wt-gated-peek">name your own first</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="wt-lead">Then compare. Every one of these is a claim the table would have made happily without the missing rows in it.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The average rating reads too high:</strong> the runs that never finished are the runs that scored low, and the players who quit for good are not in the table at all.</li>
            <li><strong>The section times read too fast:</strong> a player who stopped mid-cave never timed the section that stopped them, so the file's times only describe the players who kept going.</li>
            <li><strong>The difficulty curve reads too flat:</strong> the hardest section is the one where people stopped, and stopping is the one thing the file has no row for.</li>
        </ul>
    </details>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — a fair-data fix, in pseudocode.</strong>
    Nothing here compiles or runs: this is the exam's arrow-and-DISPLAY
    notation used to write down what happens to the rows. It is data
    reasoning, not a program. Design the fix the exam wants — a
    filtered average that also REPORTS what it excluded:</p>
    <pre><code>kept ← [row FOR row IN runs IF row.completed_boss = TRUE]
dropped ← [row FOR row IN runs IF row.completed_boss = FALSE]
DISPLAY("avg win time: " + AVERAGE([row.time_seconds FOR row IN kept]))
DISPLAY("excluded: " + LENGTH(dropped) + " runs")</code></pre>
    <p>The second DISPLAY is the ethics: an aggregate that hides its
    filter is a claim wearing a lab coat.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — algorithmic bias preview.</strong> Games do
    this too: your difficulty ramp assumes a player who finished
    the tutorial. Matchmaking assumes history predicts behavior.
    Recommendation algorithms trained on biased clicks show more
    of what was already popular. Same principle, bigger
    consequences — <a href="9.10.html">9.10</a> takes it to the
    real world.</p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Name four biases and find each in a real project's data practice</li>
        <li>Explain how filtering can silently change a conclusion</li>
        <li>Write a scoped claim: what the data does and does not represent</li>
        <li>Pseudocode an aggregate that reports its own exclusions</li>
        <li>Break "is this data fair?" into parts small enough to check one at a time, and say in one line what each part does</li>
        <li>Say which earlier lesson each of those checks came from, and where you would reread it</li>
    </ul>
</div>

